{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01ALDVzgUUtHgfjfAtyLckAk","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t## 13. Tile map\n2\t\n3\t- `new E.TileMap(o = {}) → map`: Walls standing on a floor (top-down, isometric, brawler). Either { rows: [...ASCII...], legend: { '#': 1, '@': 'hero' }, types, floorTex } (recommended) or { w, h, cells: [ids...], floor: [tags...], blocked: [0 | 1...], types, floorTex } (built in code: fill, setFloor; with no cells it starts as open floor).\n4\t- `new E.TileMap({ types })`: types[id] = { h, top, side, line, course, cut, cutH, face, roof }\n5\t- `new E.TileMap({ types[id].face })`: the wall's material (E.MATERIALS): 'brick' (default) | 'stone' | 'rock' | 'plank' | 'timber' | 'plaster' | 'hedge' | 'none' | any material id or '#hex pattern'\n6\t- `new E.TileMap({ types[id].roof })`: the top's material: 'speckle' (default) | 'plain' | 'tiles' (roof shingles) | 'slab' (paving) | 'leaves' | 'grass' | any material id or '#hex pattern'\n7\t- `new E.TileMap({ floorTex })`: floorTex(x, y, floorTag, view) -> [r, g, b] or [r, g, b, glow]\n8\t- `new E.TileMap({ floorMat, floorMats })`: a material for the floor, floorMats: { tag: material } for tagged cells (in place of floorTex)\n9\t- `new E.TileMap({ block })`: ['water', 'lava'] floor tags nobody can walk on (or put block: true in a legend entry)\n10\t- `new E.TileMap({ chunks })`: bake the floor in squares near the camera (true, false; by default a map of more than 128 x 128 cells)\n11\t- `map.arcClear(a, b, apex = 20, r = 0)`: does a jump arc from a to b (feet, { x, y, z }) clear every top on the way? The arc is a parabola peaking apex above the higher end; r > 0 also checks a track on each side of it. Jumps, throws, aerial takedowns\n12\t- `map.block(cx, cy, on = true)`: mark a cell blocked or free at runtime (a bridge appears, lava cools)\n13\t- `map.bounds(view)`: projected pixel bounds of the whole map for this view, { x0, y0, x1, y1 } with an 8-pixel margin (camera clamping: game.cam.bounds = v => map.bounds(v))\n14\t- `map.cell(cx, cy)`: the wall type id at a cell (0 = floor, -1 outside the map)\n15\t- `map.center(cx, cy)`: the world point at a cell's center\n16\t- `map.collide(b)`: push a circle { x, y, r } out of walls. If the body has a number z, walls lower than z + step (default 2) do not block it (it is standing or jumping above them). Returns true when it touched a wall.\n17\t- `map.drawFloor(r)`: draw the baked floor (first thing in draw, after r.sky); walls come from queueWalls(r)\n18\t- `map.drop(from, dir, o = {})`: what lies past the edge ahead: the first drop of more than step within reach (default 12) of `from` in direction dir, and the ground a body of its radius lands on just past it: { x, y, z, fall } or null (no edge, or a wall first). AI that won't walk off roofs, hop-downs, fall warnings\n19\t- `map.fill(cx, cy, w, h, id)`: put wall type id in every cell of a w x h block of cells (0 clears them): build maps in code\n20\t- `map.find(tag)`: first spawn with this tag ({ x, y, z, cx, cy }), or null\n21\t- `map.findAll(tag)`: every spawn with this tag\n22\t- `map.floorAt(x, y)`: floor tag from the legend ({ floor: 'water' }) at a world point, or null\n23\t- `map.floorBytes()` †: the bytes the baked floors hold now (each view's, chunks and the lighting's canvases counted): what a big map's floor costs in memory\n24\t- `map.groundAt(x, y, r = 0, z = 1e9, step = 2)`: highest surface under a circle footprint that a body at height z can stand on (floor = 0)\n25\t- `map.height(cx, cy)`: a cell's top: its wall's h, 0 on the floor, 1e9 outside the map (the height rules: E.HEIGHTS)\n26\t- `map.heightAt(x, y)`: wall height at a world point (0 on the floor)\n27\t- `map.isFront(cx, cy, view)`: is wall cell (cx, cy) in front of what the camera looks at, so cutaway cuts it down to its type's cutH (6 by default) in this view? A game may replace it on its map (map.isFront = (cx, cy, view) => ...)\n28\t- `map.landing(from, dir, o = {})`: where a jump from `from` in direction dir lands across a gap: past the first drop ahead (a top more than step below its feet), the nearest spot min..max away that rises at most rise and falls at most drop from its feet, stands above the gap's bottom, has room to stand, and that a jump arc peaking apex above the higher end clears (arcClear). { x, y, z, d } or null (a wall before any gap, or nowhere to land). o: { min: 35, max: 83, rise: 11, drop: 18, apex: 20 }\n29\t- `map.ledge(from, dir, o = {})`: a ledge to climb: in direction dir from `from`, the nearest top that rises min..rise above its feet within reach, with room to stand past the edge, looking straight ahead and spread radians to each side (a gap first means a jump: landing). { x, y, z (the spot to stand on top), rise, facing (the angle climbed), edge: [x, y] (where the hands grip) } or null. o: { reach: 18, min: 4, rise: 82, spread: .28 }\n30\t- `map.los(x0, y0, x1, y1)`: line of sight across the ground (true if nothing solid in between). It ignores heights: sight() is the one that sees over a low wall and from a roof\n31\t- `map.pick(sx, sy, at = E.current)`: the cell under a screen point on a map with heights (tactics, strategy, board games): the first top or wall face the line of sight meets, front to back, in any view, turn and zoom. sx, sy: the HUD's pixels (input.clicked(), input.pointer()); at: the game whose view and camera drew it (E.current by default) or { view, ix, iy }. Returns { cx, cy, x, y, z, face: 'top' | 'side' } (the world point it meets) or null off the map. Cells only, as height() has them (not props, actors, or walls cut away)\n32\t- `map.queueWalls(r)`: queue every visible wall box. Call once per frame after drawFloor.\n33\t- `map.ray(a, b, o = {})`: the first cell top that rises above the straight segment from a to b ({ x, y, z } world points; o.eye and o.target lift each end): { clear, t (0..1 along it), x, y, z } where it stops (b's point when clear). It walks every cell the segment crosses, not samples; a top level with the segment doesn't block it, and outside the map is solid\n34\t- `map.set(cx, cy, v)`: put wall type v at a cell (0 opens it: a door, a broken wall); bumps version, so caches and flow fields rebuild\n35\t- `map.setFloor(cx, cy, tag, block)`: give a cell a floor tag ('water', 'grass', what floorTex and floorAt read), and block it (deep water, lava) or not\n36\t- `map.sight(a, b, o = {})`: can a's eyes (z + eye) see b's chest (z + chest)? E.HEIGHTS gives both unless o ({ eye, target }) or the bodies' own eye and chest say otherwise. A guard sees over a planter lower than its eyes, and down from a roof\n37\t- `map.solid(id, x, y, z = 0, o = {})` †: make a shape solid in this map: map.solid(id | entry, x, y, z, o) with r.shape's placement (facing, scale, flip, params) -> the solid ({ x, y (the footprint's middle), z, top, hx, hy, c, s, shape, px, py (where it was placed) }; map.solids holds them; what stands on one sorts after it). A body overlapping its span in height is pushed out of its footprint; one coming down onto it lands on its top; one wholly below passes under. Register solids before the bodies' updates; drawing never sets them\n38\t- `map.solidAt(x, y)`: solidCell at a world point\n39\t- `map.solidCell(cx, cy)`: true where a cell has a wall (or is outside the map); walkable() also counts blocked floor\n40\t- `map.standable(x, y, z, r = 5, step = HEIGHTS.step)`: can a body of radius r stand at (x, y) with its feet at z? Nothing under its footprint rises above z + step (it would collide), no blocked floor (water, lava) is under it at floor level, and a top within step below z holds it up\n41\t- `map.toCell(x, y)`: world point -> [cx, cy] cell (center(cx, cy) goes back)\n42\t- `map.walkable(cx, cy)`: true where a body can walk: no wall and not a blocked floor (water, lava). Pathfinding uses this.\n43\t- `map.wall(from, dir, o = {})` †: the wall face a body touches or faces: from `from`, in direction dir (an angle or [dx, dy]), the first point within reach whose top rises more than a step above its feet. { x, y (on the face), nx, ny (the face's normal, out toward the body), top (how high it rises there), bottom (the floor at its foot), facing (the angle into it) } or null. Cells give axis faces; a map.solid box, the face back along dir. o: { reach: from.r + 4 }\n44\t- `map.T`: the tile size in world units (16)\n45\t- `map.ao`: contact shadow width (world units) along the foot of walls; 0 turns it off\n46\t- `map.blocked`: cells nobody can walk on (deep water, lava, pits): legend { floor: 'water', block: true } or TileMap { block: ['water'] }\n47\t- `map.cells`: the wall type id of every cell, row by row (index cy * w + cx; change one with set)\n48\t- `map.chunks` †: the floor baked in squares of 128 screen pixels as the camera comes to them (its colors and the GPU lighting's canvas both), the far ones let go, so a 256 x 256-cell map costs what the screen shows; a change to cells re-bakes only the squares over them. True, false, or by default when the map has more than 128 x 128 cells\n49\t- `map.cutaway`: walls between the camera and the floor drop low (types with cut: true; false turns it off)\n50\t- `map.floorMat` †: a material (E.MATERIALS id or '#hex pattern') for every floor cell\n51\t- `map.floorMats` †: { tag: material } for the cells with that floor tag, over floorMat (untagged cells have the tag null). Either stands in for floorTex\n52\t- `map.floorTags`: each cell's floor tag from the legend ('water', 'lava', ...), row by row, or null for a map built from cells\n53\t- `map.floorTex`: floorTex(x, y, tag, view) -> [r, g, b], the floor's color per pixel\n54\t- `map.floors`: the baked floor images by view (floors = {} re-bakes them after floorTex changes)\n55\t- `map.h`, `map.w`: the map's size in cells\n56\t- `map.light`: null shades wall faces with the shapes' light (from the viewer's upper left: a face toward it lit, the other face of a corner a shade darker, in every view), or a direction [x, y, 0] the light comes from\n57\t- `map.outside`: the ground beyond the map (ground views): undefined continues the map's floor past its edge, darkening into one color that fills the rest of the screen, a color ('#2a2630') fades into that, false leaves it empty\n58\t- `map.shimmer` †: animated sparkle on floor cells tagged 'water' or 'lava'\n59\t- `map.spawns`: spawn points from the ASCII rows: { tag, ch, cx, cy, x, y, z } (x, y = cell center)\n60\t- `map.types`: the wall types by id ({ h, top, side, face, roof, cut, ... })\n61\t- `map.version = 0`: counts every change to cells and blocked cells (set, block), so caches and flow fields know to rebuild\n62\t- `E.parseLevel(rows, legend = {})`: Parse an ASCII level (top row first; every character is one tile). legend maps a character to a number -> tile / wall type id (0 = empty) a string -> a spawn tag; the cell stays empty and spawns[] gets { tag, ch, cx, cy } an object -> any of { tile: id, floor: 'water', spawn: 'hero', block: true } (block: nobody can walk on this cell) '.' and ' ' are empty unless the legend says otherwise. Unknown characters warn once and become empty.\n63\t\n64\t-----\n65\t174:- `E.pick(list)`: a random item of list (Math.random)\n66\t228:- `view.set(yaw, pitch, scale = 1, zBoost = 1)`: change the angles and scale in place (game.setView and rotateView use their own copies); returns the view\n67\t426:- `r.pick(sx, sy)` †: what was drawn under a screen point (the HUD's pixels: input.clicked(), input.pointer()) in the last frame, front to back: the pick values of the actors, sprites, props and queued draws given one (r.actor(x, y, z, fn, { pick: unit })), each where its pixels went (an actor's drawn bounds; others' box, or a body's size round its point). [] for none\n68\t430:- `r.shape(id, x, y, z = 0, o = {})` †: Queue a shape standing at (x, y, z), feet on the point, depth sorted like an actor: r.shape(id | entry, x, y, z, o). o: { facing (radians, where its front points; default PI/2, south, as written), scale, mats: { slot: material }, flip (mirror it x to -x before facing), params (a generator's: { h: 30 }), bias, hides (x-ray silhouettes show through it: by default when it stands at least E.HEIGHTS.chest, 22, high), footprint, light (add its lights to game.lights), shadow (default true), charView (default true: steep views draw it from the characters' lower angle), flash, alpha, xray, pick (r.pick finds it) }\n69\t464:- `new E.Game({ pace })`: false draws every refresh of a fast display even when the frames can't keep up (by default it draws every other one, evenly), unless the player picks Frame rate 60\n70\t485:- `game.mouseGround()`: mouse position on the ground (world units), or null\n71\t492:- `game.resetCamera()` †: undo setZoom and rotateView: the scene's own zoom again (camera.start, or camera.tall on a screen held upright; 1 without), no turn. The player's zoom stays (settings.zoom)\n72\t494:- `game.rotateView(deg)` †: turn the camera around the vertical axis in ground views: game.rotateView(45) (degrees). Side views never turn\n73\t498:- `game.setZoom(z)` †: camera zoom on top of the view: game.setZoom(1.5) (.5 .. 3), the game's own; where the scene lets the player zoom (camera.zoom), theirs multiplies it (game.zoom is the two together). Characters, maps, props, particles and lights are drawn by code, so they re-rasterize crisply at the new size; HUD text and overlays stay the same.\n74\t557:- `game.yaw = 0` †: its turn in degrees on top of the view (rotateView)\n75\t558:- `game.zoom = 1` †: the camera's zoom now (1 = as the view is): the game's (setZoom, a scene's camera.start) times the player's (settings.zoom: the wheel, a pinch), within the scene's camera.zoom range\n76\t761:- `map.pick(sx, sy, at = E.current)`: the cell under a screen point on a map with heights (tactics, strategy, board games): the first top or wall face the line of sight meets, front to back, in any view, turn and zoom. sx, sy: the HUD's pixels (input.clicked(), input.pointer()); at: the game whose view and camera drew it (E.current by default) or { view, ix, iy }. Returns { cx, cy, x, y, z, face: 'top' | 'side' } (the world point it meets) or null off the map. Cells only, as height() has them (not props, actors, or walls cut away)\n77\t----\n78\t1487:- `E.tint(r, cells, color, alpha = .4, T = 16, height = null)`: tints on cells' tops: E.tint(r, cells ([[cx, cy], ...]), color, alpha = .4, T = 16, height(cx, cy) = 0): a move range, a path, an attack's reach, drawn on the floor (decals) where height is 0; on a raised top (a TileMap wall's) it sorts with the map: over that top, under whoever stands on it, behind what is in front\n79\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"07d72fa0-3e0a-4abc-a856-6a3bd495de67","uuid":"ccdf81bf-a7ce-4ad1-a3d7-7673aa7cead6","timestamp":"2026-11-04T00:56:30.672Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174758__x42640e/work/.research/out13.txt","content":"## 13. Tile map\n\n- `new E.TileMap(o = {}) → map`: Walls standing on a floor (top-down, isometric, brawler). Either { rows: [...ASCII...], legend: { '#': 1, '@': 'hero' }, types, floorTex } (recommended) or { w, h, cells: [ids...], floor: [tags...], blocked: [0 | 1...], types, floorTex } (built in code: fill, setFloor; with no cells it starts as open floor).\n- `new E.TileMap({ types })`: types[id] = { h, top, side, line, course, cut, cutH, face, roof }\n- `new E.TileMap({ types[id].face })`: the wall's material (E.MATERIALS): 'brick' (default) | 'stone' | 'rock' | 'plank' | 'timber' | 'plaster' | 'hedge' | 'none' | any material id or '#hex pattern'\n- `new E.TileMap({ types[id].roof })`: the top's material: 'speckle' (default) | 'plain' | 'tiles' (roof shingles) | 'slab' (paving) | 'leaves' | 'grass' | any material id or '#hex pattern'\n- `new E.TileMap({ floorTex })`: floorTex(x, y, floorTag, view) -> [r, g, b] or [r, g, b, glow]\n- `new E.TileMap({ floorMat, floorMats })`: a material for the floor, floorMats: { tag: material } for tagged cells (in place of floorTex)\n- `new E.TileMap({ block })`: ['water', 'lava'] floor tags nobody can walk on (or put block: true in a legend entry)\n- `new E.TileMap({ chunks })`: bake the floor in squares near the camera (true, false; by default a map of more than 128 x 128 cells)\n- `map.arcClear(a, b, apex = 20, r = 0)`: does a jump arc from a to b (feet, { x, y, z }) clear every top on the way? The arc is a parabola peaking apex above the higher end; r > 0 also checks a track on each side of it. Jumps, throws, aerial takedowns\n- `map.block(cx, cy, on = true)`: mark a cell blocked or free at runtime (a bridge appears, lava cools)\n- `map.bounds(view)`: projected pixel bounds of the whole map for this view, { x0, y0, x1, y1 } with an 8-pixel margin (camera clamping: game.cam.bounds = v => map.bounds(v))\n- `map.cell(cx, cy)`: the wall type id at a cell (0 = floor, -1 outside the map)\n- `map.center(cx, cy)`: the world point at a cell's center\n- `map.collide(b)`: push a circle { x, y, r } out of walls. If the body has a number z, walls lower than z + step (default 2) do not block it (it is standing or jumping above them). Returns true when it touched a wall.\n- `map.drawFloor(r)`: draw the baked floor (first thing in draw, after r.sky); walls come from queueWalls(r)\n- `map.drop(from, dir, o = {})`: what lies past the edge ahead: the first drop of more than step within reach (default 12) of `from` in direction dir, and the ground a body of its radius lands on just past it: { x, y, z, fall } or null (no edge, or a wall first). AI that won't walk off roofs, hop-downs, fall warnings\n- `map.fill(cx, cy, w, h, id)`: put wall type id in every cell of a w x h block of cells (0 clears them): build maps in code\n- `map.find(tag)`: first spawn with this tag ({ x, y, z, cx, cy }), or null\n- `map.findAll(tag)`: every spawn with this tag\n- `map.floorAt(x, y)`: floor tag from the legend ({ floor: 'water' }) at a world point, or null\n- `map.floorBytes()` †: the bytes the baked floors hold now (each view's, chunks and the lighting's canvases counted): what a big map's floor costs in memory\n- `map.groundAt(x, y, r = 0, z = 1e9, step = 2)`: highest surface under a circle footprint that a body at height z can stand on (floor = 0)\n- `map.height(cx, cy)`: a cell's top: its wall's h, 0 on the floor, 1e9 outside the map (the height rules: E.HEIGHTS)\n- `map.heightAt(x, y)`: wall height at a world point (0 on the floor)\n- `map.isFront(cx, cy, view)`: is wall cell (cx, cy) in front of what the camera looks at, so cutaway cuts it down to its type's cutH (6 by default) in this view? A game may replace it on its map (map.isFront = (cx, cy, view) => ...)\n- `map.landing(from, dir, o = {})`: where a jump from `from` in direction dir lands across a gap: past the first drop ahead (a top more than step below its feet), the nearest spot min..max away that rises at most rise and falls at most drop from its feet, stands above the gap's bottom, has room to stand, and that a jump arc peaking apex above the higher end clears (arcClear). { x, y, z, d } or null (a wall before any gap, or nowhere to land). o: { min: 35, max: 83, rise: 11, drop: 18, apex: 20 }\n- `map.ledge(from, dir, o = {})`: a ledge to climb: in direction dir from `from`, the nearest top that rises min..rise above its feet within reach, with room to stand past the edge, looking straight ahead and spread radians to each side (a gap first means a jump: landing). { x, y, z (the spot to stand on top), rise, facing (the angle climbed), edge: [x, y] (where the hands grip) } or null. o: { reach: 18, min: 4, rise: 82, spread: .28 }\n- `map.los(x0, y0, x1, y1)`: line of sight across the ground (true if nothing solid in between). It ignores heights: sight() is the one that sees over a low wall and from a roof\n- `map.pick(sx, sy, at = E.current)`: the cell under a screen point on a map with heights (tactics, strategy, board games): the first top or wall face the line of sight meets, front to back, in any view, turn and zoom. sx, sy: the HUD's pixels (input.clicked(), input.pointer()); at: the game whose view and camera drew it (E.current by default) or { view, ix, iy }. Returns { cx, cy, x, y, z, face: 'top' | 'side' } (the world point it meets) or null off the map. Cells only, as height() has them (not props, actors, or walls cut away)\n- `map.queueWalls(r)`: queue every visible wall box. Call once per frame after drawFloor.\n- `map.ray(a, b, o = {})`: the first cell top that rises above the straight segment from a to b ({ x, y, z } world points; o.eye and o.target lift each end): { clear, t (0..1 along it), x, y, z } where it stops (b's point when clear). It walks every cell the segment crosses, not samples; a top level with the segment doesn't block it, and outside the map is solid\n- `map.set(cx, cy, v)`: put wall type v at a cell (0 opens it: a door, a broken wall); bumps version, so caches and flow fields rebuild\n- `map.setFloor(cx, cy, tag, block)`: give a cell a floor tag ('water', 'grass', what floorTex and floorAt read), and block it (deep water, lava) or not\n- `map.sight(a, b, o = {})`: can a's eyes (z + eye) see b's chest (z + chest)? E.HEIGHTS gives both unless o ({ eye, target }) or the bodies' own eye and chest say otherwise. A guard sees over a planter lower than its eyes, and down from a roof\n- `map.solid(id, x, y, z = 0, o = {})` †: make a shape solid in this map: map.solid(id | entry, x, y, z, o) with r.shape's placement (facing, scale, flip, params) -> the solid ({ x, y (the footprint's middle), z, top, hx, hy, c, s, shape, px, py (where it was placed) }; map.solids holds them; what stands on one sorts after it). A body overlapping its span in height is pushed out of its footprint; one coming down onto it lands on its top; one wholly below passes under. Register solids before the bodies' updates; drawing never sets them\n- `map.solidAt(x, y)`: solidCell at a world point\n- `map.solidCell(cx, cy)`: true where a cell has a wall (or is outside the map); walkable() also counts blocked floor\n- `map.standable(x, y, z, r = 5, step = HEIGHTS.step)`: can a body of radius r stand at (x, y) with its feet at z? Nothing under its footprint rises above z + step (it would collide), no blocked floor (water, lava) is under it at floor level, and a top within step below z holds it up\n- `map.toCell(x, y)`: world point -> [cx, cy] cell (center(cx, cy) goes back)\n- `map.walkable(cx, cy)`: true where a body can walk: no wall and not a blocked floor (water, lava). Pathfinding uses this.\n- `map.wall(from, dir, o = {})` †: the wall face a body touches or faces: from `from`, in direction dir (an angle or [dx, dy]), the first point within reach whose top rises more than a step above its feet. { x, y (on the face), nx, ny (the face's normal, out toward the body), top (how high it rises there), bottom (the floor at its foot), facing (the angle into it) } or null. Cells give axis faces; a map.solid box, the face back along dir. o: { reach: from.r + 4 }\n- `map.T`: the tile size in world units (16)\n- `map.ao`: contact shadow width (world units) along the foot of walls; 0 turns it off\n- `map.blocked`: cells nobody can walk on (deep water, lava, pits): legend { floor: 'water', block: true } or TileMap { block: ['water'] }\n- `map.cells`: the wall type id of every cell, row by row (index cy * w + cx; change one with set)\n- `map.chunks` †: the floor baked in squares of 128 screen pixels as the camera comes to them (its colors and the GPU lighting's canvas both), the far ones let go, so a 256 x 256-cell map costs what the screen shows; a change to cells re-bakes only the squares over them. True, false, or by default when the map has more than 128 x 128 cells\n- `map.cutaway`: walls between the camera and the floor drop low (types with cut: true; false turns it off)\n- `map.floorMat` †: a material (E.MATERIALS id or '#hex pattern') for every floor cell\n- `map.floorMats` †: { tag: material } for the cells with that floor tag, over floorMat (untagged cells have the tag null). Either stands in for floorTex\n- `map.floorTags`: each cell's floor tag from the legend ('water', 'lava', ...), row by row, or null for a map built from cells\n- `map.floorTex`: floorTex(x, y, tag, view) -> [r, g, b], the floor's color per pixel\n- `map.floors`: the baked floor images by view (floors = {} re-bakes them after floorTex changes)\n- `map.h`, `map.w`: the map's size in cells\n- `map.light`: null shades wall faces with the shapes' light (from the viewer's upper left: a face toward it lit, the other face of a corner a shade darker, in every view), or a direction [x, y, 0] the light comes from\n- `map.outside`: the ground beyond the map (ground views): undefined continues the map's floor past its edge, darkening into one color that fills the rest of the screen, a color ('#2a2630') fades into that, false leaves it empty\n- `map.shimmer` †: animated sparkle on floor cells tagged 'water' or 'lava'\n- `map.spawns`: spawn points from the ASCII rows: { tag, ch, cx, cy, x, y, z } (x, y = cell center)\n- `map.types`: the wall types by id ({ h, top, side, face, roof, cut, ... })\n- `map.version = 0`: counts every change to cells and blocked cells (set, block), so caches and flow fields know to rebuild\n- `E.parseLevel(rows, legend = {})`: Parse an ASCII level (top row first; every character is one tile). legend maps a character to a number -> tile / wall type id (0 = empty) a string -> a spawn tag; the cell stays empty and spawns[] gets { tag, ch, cx, cy } an object -> any of { tile: id, floor: 'water', spawn: 'hero', block: true } (block: nobody can walk on this cell) '.' and ' ' are empty unless the legend says otherwise. Unknown characters warn once and become empty.\n\n-----\n174:- `E.pick(list)`: a random item of list (Math.random)\n228:- `view.set(yaw, pitch, scale = 1, zBoost = 1)`: change the angles and scale in place (game.setView and rotateView use their own copies); returns the view\n426:- `r.pick(sx, sy)` †: what was drawn under a screen point (the HUD's pixels: input.clicked(), input.pointer()) in the last frame, front to back: the pick values of the actors, sprites, props and queued draws given one (r.actor(x, y, z, fn, { pick: unit })), each where its pixels went (an actor's drawn bounds; others' box, or a body's size round its point). [] for none\n430:- `r.shape(id, x, y, z = 0, o = {})` †: Queue a shape standing at (x, y, z), feet on the point, depth sorted like an actor: r.shape(id | entry, x, y, z, o). o: { facing (radians, where its front points; default PI/2, south, as written), scale, mats: { slot: material }, flip (mirror it x to -x before facing), params (a generator's: { h: 30 }), bias, hides (x-ray silhouettes show through it: by default when it stands at least E.HEIGHTS.chest, 22, high), footprint, light (add its lights to game.lights), shadow (default true), charView (default true: steep views draw it from the characters' lower angle), flash, alpha, xray, pick (r.pick finds it) }\n464:- `new E.Game({ pace })`: false draws every refresh of a fast display even when the frames can't keep up (by default it draws every other one, evenly), unless the player picks Frame rate 60\n485:- `game.mouseGround()`: mouse position on the ground (world units), or null\n492:- `game.resetCamera()` †: undo setZoom and rotateView: the scene's own zoom again (camera.start, or camera.tall on a screen held upright; 1 without), no turn. The player's zoom stays (settings.zoom)\n494:- `game.rotateView(deg)` †: turn the camera around the vertical axis in ground views: game.rotateView(45) (degrees). Side views never turn\n498:- `game.setZoom(z)` †: camera zoom on top of the view: game.setZoom(1.5) (.5 .. 3), the game's own; where the scene lets the player zoom (camera.zoom), theirs multiplies it (game.zoom is the two together). Characters, maps, props, particles and lights are drawn by code, so they re-rasterize crisply at the new size; HUD text and overlays stay the same.\n557:- `game.yaw = 0` †: its turn in degrees on top of the view (rotateView)\n558:- `game.zoom = 1` †: the camera's zoom now (1 = as the view is): the game's (setZoom, a scene's camera.start) times the player's (settings.zoom: the wheel, a pinch), within the scene's camera.zoom range\n761:- `map.pick(sx, sy, at = E.current)`: the cell under a screen point on a map with heights (tactics, strategy, board games): the first top or wall face the line of sight meets, front to back, in any view, turn and zoom. sx, sy: the HUD's pixels (input.clicked(), input.pointer()); at: the game whose view and camera drew it (E.current by default) or { view, ix, iy }. Returns { cx, cy, x, y, z, face: 'top' | 'side' } (the world point it meets) or null off the map. Cells only, as height() has them (not props, actors, or walls cut away)\n----\n1487:- `E.tint(r, cells, color, alpha = .4, T = 16, height = null)`: tints on cells' tops: E.tint(r, cells ([[cx, cy], ...]), color, alpha = .4, T = 16, height(cx, cy) = 0): a move range, a path, an attack's reach, drawn on the floor (decals) where height is 0; on a raised top (a TileMap wall's) it sorts with the map: over that top, under whoever stands on it, behind what is in front\n","numLines":79,"startLine":1,"totalLines":79}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01ALDVzgUUtHgfjfAtyLckAk","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
