{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgh3iLZcQUqsztjt5hF5i","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Fj2Paztwvof52UsiH2Sy17","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174758__x42640e/work/PLAN.md","limit":330},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":739,"cache_read_input_tokens":570070,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":739},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"07d72fa0-3e0a-4abc-a856-6a3bd495de67","uuid":"b75e4927-83e2-42ac-af67-4ca1dfb33f3c","timestamp":"2026-11-04T01:49:47.911Z","request_id":"req_011Cgh3iLEm4wwxk1wPSQzp3","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01Fj2Paztwvof52UsiH2Sy17":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174758__x42640e/work/PLAN.md","limit":330}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Fj2Paztwvof52UsiH2Sy17","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Brigantime: The Legend of Forseasons\n2\t\n3\tBuild plan for a feature-complete demo inspired by **Brigandine: The Legend of Forsena** (Hearty Robin, PlayStation;\n4\tAtlus in North America, 1998). It is for the developers who will build the game from `TASK.md` and this file, who may be\n5\tother AI models working alone. Every number here is a starting value that the dev-mode sliders can tune.\n6\t\n7\t**How the title was picked (as TASK.md asks):** I brainstormed 50 riffs on the source's name, then a real random number\n8\tgenerator (Python's `secrets.randbelow(50)`, which draws from the OS) picked #21 on 2026-11-03 at 17:57:56. The title then\n9\tshaped the design. *Brigan-time* is the name of an age of war and of a clock that is running out. *Forseasons* stands for\n10\tfour seasons and four realms: the seasons drive the calendar, the elements, the terrain and the story.\n11\t\n12\t---\n13\t\n14\t## 0. At a glance\n15\t\n16\t**Pitch.** The Keeper of the Wheel sleeps in Meridian, and the Great Clock that turns the seasons is failing. Four\n17\tseason-realms, Verdance (Spring), Solmark (Summer), Amberlyn (Autumn) and Hoarfast (Winter), race one another and the\n18\tusurping Hourless Dominion to reach the Calendar City before the Thirteenth Month, when the Gray Knight will stop time\n19\tforever. The player picks a realm and musters Wheelsworn knights who bind monsters to their banners. Castles yield the\n20\tmana to summon beasts. Knights go questing, and armies march on neighbouring castles season by season. Every attack\n21\tbecomes a turn-based hex battle of up to three troops a side, fought within twelve bells. Each season changes the land\n22\t(spring floods, summer fires, autumn gales, winter ice) and the strength of every army.\n23\t\n24\t**Core loop (one sentence, write it at the top of `src/00-core.js`):** each season, read the tidings, muster your castles\n25\t(summon, bind, move, quest, equip, promote), declare your marches, then fight the battles they cause, growing knights and\n26\tmonsters until you can take Meridian and defeat the Gray Knight before the Hour-Candle burns out.\n27\t\n28\t**Genre:** turn-based grand strategy with a tactical RPG battle layer.\n29\t\n30\t**Perspective:**\n31\t- Campaign: a three-quarter overhead diorama of the continent (the engine's `threequarter` view, north up, pan and zoom).\n32\t- Battles: a tilted overhead \"board\" view of a hex battlefield (a custom `E.View`, yaw 0, pitch 50), rotatable in 60°\n33\t  steps and zoomable, with the engine's `iso` view as the alternate (`V`).\n34\t- Story events: side-on stage scenes (the engine's `brawler` view) with live portraits in the dialog box.\n35\t- Title and attract mode: a diorama of the four seasons around Meridian's clock tower.\n36\t\n37\t**Scope (all of it ships complete, with nothing stubbed):**\n38\t\n39\t| Content | Count |\n40\t|---|---|\n41\t| Continent / castles / roads | 1 (Forseason) / 25 / 36 |\n42\t| Realms | 4 playable + the Hourless Dominion (AI only) + 4 neutral brigand castles |\n43\t| Knights | 36: 4 playable rulers, 16 realm knights, 6 Dominion knights (ruler included), the Gray Knight, 5 free knights, 4 brigand captains |\n44\t| Knight classes | 23: 5 lines × 3 tiers, plus 8 unique classes |\n45\t| Monster species | 30, each with 3 forms (tier 1 → 2 → 3) |\n46\t| Skills and spells / effects / items | 90 / 11 / 33 |\n47\t| Battle maps | 13 hand-made hex maps (all checked by script), dressed per region and changed per season |\n48\t| Story | about 40 events, 4 good endings, 3 kinds of bad ending, a tutorial battle |\n49\t| Modes | Campaign (1–4 players, hot-seat), Skirmish (2–4 sides, local humans or CPU), AI Hub shared-world skirmish (2–4 online), attract mode, dev mode |\n50\t\n51\tA campaign lasts at most 16 seasons. A first win on Normal should take 2–3 hours with battles played by hand, or about\n52\t45 minutes with battles delegated.\n53\t\n54\t**Deliverables (exact names):**\n55\t- Dev folder: `BrigantimeTheLegendOfForseasons-BrigandineTheLegendOfForsena-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174758/` (called\n56\t  `<DEV>` below). GameTitle and SOURCE are written in PascalCase with the punctuation removed.\n57\t- The game: `BrigantimeTheLegendOfForseasons-BrigandineTheLegendOfForsena-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174758.html`, one\n58\t  self-contained file in this folder's root, beside `TASK.md`.\n59\t- `exit-interview.json` in this folder's root, in TASK.md's exact shape (§15).\n60\t- The About/Credits screen and the final chat reply carry Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt\n61\t  Feedback (§10.4, §15). Plan: `PLAN.md`.\n62\t\n63\t---\n64\t\n65\t## 1. From the source to this game\n66\t\n67\t### 1.1 What makes Brigandine distinctive, and what it becomes here\n68\t\n69\t| Source trait | Mechanic in Brigantime |\n70\t|---|---|\n71\t| Months of war, each with an Organize phase and an Attack phase | Seasons (Spring → Summer → Autumn → Winter), each with **Tidings** (news), **Muster** (organize) and **March** (attack) |\n72\t| A continent of castles held by rival nations; pick one and conquer | Forseason: 25 castles on a wheel-shaped map, 4 playable season-realms, the Dominion at the hub, brigand castles between |\n73\t| Rune Knights lead troops of monsters; Rune Power limits how many | **Wheelsworn knights** lead a troop of up to 6 monsters; a knight's **Bond** caps the troop's total bond cost |\n74\t| Castles make mana; summoning costs mana; monsters cost upkeep | The same, plus seasonal yields and a **summon circle** per castle (taking castles unlocks new species) |\n75\t| Quests: a knight leaves for a while; mostly random help or harm | Quests to named sites with an outcome table (treasure, beasts, wanderers, mana, training, mishap) and short vignettes |\n76\t| Hex battles, one unit per hex, up to 3 troops a side; the attacker has 12 turns | Hex battles of up to 12 **bells** (a clock face counts them); an attacker knight on the Keep at the last bell also wins |\n77\t| A troop is beaten when its leader falls or retreats; a fallen ruler routs the nation | The same rules |\n78\t| Rune Area: monsters outside it weaken and are easier to capture | **Ward**: a radius around each knight; outside it monsters lose 20% ATK, DEF and INT and are easier to capture |\n79\t| Monsters at 0 HP die for good; knights are out for a month | Monsters at 0 HP die for good; knights are wounded and miss the next season |\n80\t| Elements red, blue, green, white and black, with opposites | **Seasons are the elements**: Spring opposes Autumn, Summer opposes Winter; **Hour** (the enemy's timeless element) stands apart |\n81\t| Scale: big monsters struggle to hit small, quick ones | Size classes S, M and L change hit chance (a dragon swinging at a pixie: −30%) |\n82\t| Spells can't be cast after moving; some special attacks can | The same: spells need planted feet; specials marked *mobile* work after moving |\n83\t| Knights change class; monsters promote | 5 class lines × 3 tiers plus unique ruler classes; every species has 3 forms |\n84\t| Brief, earnest story vignettes between turns, a path per nation | The same, with choices that set flags, recruit knights or change the AI |\n85\t| Grand Edition: up to 6 human nations; a final boss after conquest | Hot-seat for 1–4 humans; AI Hub online skirmish; the Gray Knight in the Clockwork Sanctum |\n86\t| *New, from the title* | **Seasonal Tide** (the season's element hits harder), **seasonal terrain** (floods, fires, gales, ice), the **Hour-Candle** deadline, the **Season Dials** puzzle in the final battle |\n87\t\n88\t### 1.2 Originality guardrails\n89\t- The title riff is the only nod to the source's name. Never use the source's proper names: Forsena, Almekia, Esgares,\n90\t  Norgard, Caerleon, Iscalio, Leonia, Padstow, Lance, Zemeckis, Cador, Henguist, Vaynard, Lyonesse, Dryst, Cai, or any\n91\t  other source character, castle or monster name.\n92\t- The world, map, characters, plot, art, music and UI layouts are our own. Knight names come from public-domain\n93\t  Arthurian legend or are invented, which matches the source's Arthurian flavour without copying its cast.\n94\t- Monsters are generic folklore types (wolves, dragons, golems, pixies) drawn by our own rigs.\n95\t\n96\t---\n97\t\n98\t## 2. Folders, build and the working environment\n99\t\n100\t```\n101\t<this folder>/                      TASK.md, STAGE.md, PLAN.md, the built .html, exit-interview.json\n102\t  <DEV>/\n103\t    engine/        a copy of my-3d2dge dist/handoff (v0.85.2): my-3d2dge.js (full engine, every module), modules/,\n104\t                   tools/ (check, filmstrip, replay, asset-*), docs/ASSETS.md, kits/ (reference only), package.json\n105\t    engine-ext/    new engine-level code: beasts.js (the Beast rig module, §7.5); changes to engine/ are edits in place\n106\t    vendor/hub/    AI Hub: session.js, harness.html, bridge.js, round.js, copied unmodified\n107\t    src/           the game: numbered files, concatenated in order into one IIFE (§3.2)\n108\t    tests/         run.mjs (the one test command) and the suites t-*.mjs\n109\t    tools/         build.mjs, simulate.mjs (balance runs), hub-check.mjs (harness driver)\n110\t    dist/          build output: game.js, index.html (the dev page), hub/ (harness files + a copy of the game)\n111\t    ENGINE-CHANGES.md   every change to engine/ or engine-ext/, and why (it feeds Engine Feedback)\n112\t```\n113\t\n114\t- **Get the engine:** `git clone --depth 1 --filter=blob:none --sparse https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge.git` into\n115\t  a new empty directory, then `git sparse-checkout set dist/handoff`, then copy `dist/handoff/*` into `<DEV>/engine/`. Read\n116\t  its `README.md` and `API.md` whole; read the parts of `AI_GUIDE.md` named here; grep `API-INDEX.md` for names. Do the\n117\t  same for `https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub` with `lib/hub`, copied into `<DEV>/vendor/hub/`, and read its\n118\t  `README.md` whole.\n119\t- **Build (`node tools/build.mjs`):**\n120\t  1. Concatenate `src/*.js` in name order inside `(() => { 'use strict'; const E = My3D2dge, G = {}; ... })();` and write\n121\t     `dist/game.js`. Each file attaches what it defines to `G`; there are no other globals.\n122\t  2. Write `dist/index.html`, which loads `../engine/my-3d2dge.js`, `../engine-ext/beasts.js` and `game.js`, for\n123\t     development.\n124\t  3. Write the single file in this order: `<!doctype html><html lang=\"en\"><head><meta charset=\"utf-8\">`, then\n125\t     `<script>` holding `vendor/hub/session.js` verbatim (the first script, unmodified), then the hub meta tags (§11.2),\n126\t     the viewport meta, `<title>Brigantime: The Legend of Forseasons</title>` and the page style. The `<body>` holds the\n127\t     `E.bootSplash('BRIGANTIME')` markup and `<canvas id=\"screen\">`, then one `<script>` each for the engine,\n128\t     engine-ext and the game. Escape every `</script` inside inlined code as `<\\/script`.\n129\t  4. Fail the build if `src/` contains `TODO`, `FIXME`, `XXX`, `placeholder` or `lorem`, or if any asset URL is not a\n130\t     data URI.\n131\t- **Play:** open the single HTML file from `file://`. During development, open `dist/index.html`.\n132\t- **This machine:** at planning time `/tmp` was a full 9.8 GB tmpfs with 0 MB free, so command output written there\n133\t  gets lost. Set `TMPDIR=<DEV>/.tmp` before running Playwright or Chromium, and send long command output to files on the\n134\t  main disk. Delete nothing in `/tmp` that you did not create.\n135\t- **Servers** (only the hub harness needs one): run `python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` from `<DEV>/dist/hub/`\n136\t  and read the port it prints. Fetch the game page and check that its `<title>` is ours before trusting a result. Stop\n137\t  the server by its own PID only; never use `pkill`, `killall`, `fuser -k`, or kill whatever holds a port. Other agents\n138\t  share this machine and directory tree, so read only your own folder and the engine and hub sources.\n139\t\n140\t---\n141\t\n142\t## 3. Architecture\n143\t\n144\t### 3.1 Layers\n145\t\n146\t```\n147\t data registries ──► simulations (pure, deterministic, JSON state) ──► events ──► presentation (scenes, art, audio, UI)\n148\t        ▲                     ▲                                                        │\n149\t        │                     └──── commands ◄── AI (campaign, battle) / input (pad, keys, mouse) / net (AI Hub)\n150\t```\n151\t\n152\t- **Data.** Every content item is registered once with `G.def(kind, id, spec)`, then read with `G.get(kind, id)` and\n153\t  `G.all(kind)`. The kinds are `element, terrain, effect, skill, class, monster, knight, item, castle, map, site, event,\n154\t  realm, song, voice`. A validation suite checks that every id a spec refers to exists. Content never edits the code\n155\t  that runs it.\n156\t- **Simulations.** `G.BattleSim` and `G.CampaignSim` keep their state as plain JSON. They never touch the engine, the\n157\t  DOM, the clock or `Math.random`. Each one offers `legal(...)`, `apply(cmd) → events[]`, `forecast(...)` (battle),\n158\t  `serialize()`, `G.*Sim.load(json)` and `hash()`. Randomness comes from `G.Rng` (mulberry32), whose state lives inside\n159\t  the sim state, so the same seed and the same commands always give the same events. Tests assert this.\n160\t- **AI.** Pure functions turn a sim state into commands. They serve CPU realms, Delegate (auto-resolve), the attract\n161\t  mode, the hub host for players who time out, and the playthrough bot.\n162\t- **Presentation.** Engine scenes read sim state and play the event stream through `G.EventPlayer`: a queue in which each\n163\t  event type has a player that animates it and reports when it is done. Scenes turn input into commands. Presentation\n164\t  never writes sim state directly, so the battle the player watches is the same battle auto-resolve would compute.\n165\t- **Net.** The AI Hub layer wraps `BattleSim` in a protocol where the host's copy is the authority (§11).\n166\t\n167\t### 3.2 Source files (concatenated in this order)\n168\t\n169\t| File | Holds |\n170\t|---|---|\n171\t| `00-core.js` | `G` namespace, the core-loop line, `G.def/get/all`, `G.Rng`, hex helpers (`G.hex.center`, `toCell`, `corners`, `edgeDist`, `dirTo`, screen-direction → neighbour), the event bus `G.on/emit`, `G.tune` (live tunables) and `G.TUNE_DEFAULTS` |\n172\t| `01-data-rules.js` | elements and their relations, seasons and the Tide, terrain types and season transforms, size table, effects (statuses, buffs, debuffs) |\n173\t| `02-data-skills.js` | every skill and spell (§6.7) |\n174\t| `03-data-classes.js` | knight classes, growth, class changes (§6.6) |\n175\t| `04-data-monsters.js` | the bestiary: 30 species × 3 forms, rig specs, voices (§6.8) |\n176\t| `05-data-items.js` | items (§6.9) |\n177\t| `06-data-knights.js` | every knight: class, level, element, look, voice, personality (§6.5) |\n178\t| `07-data-world.js` | realms, castles, roads, circles, sites, starting state (§6.1–6.4, §6.11) |\n179\t| `08-data-maps.js` | the 13 battle maps as ASCII rows plus their dressing (§6.10) |\n180\t| `09-data-story.js` | events: triggers, conditions, `E.Story` nodes, effects, endings, rival lines (§6.12) |\n181\t| `10-data-about.js` | the About/Credits text: Source Information and the three feedback lists (§10.4) |\n182\t| `20-sim-battle.js` | `BattleSim`: setup and deployment, turn order, legality, actions, formulas, Ward, capture, bells, Keep, wins, EXP and levels, forecast |\n183\t| `21-ai-battle.js` | the battle planner (§5.13) and auto-resolve |\n184\t| `22-sim-campaign.js` | `CampaignSim`: calendar, phases, economy, summon, bind, move, quest, equip, promote, marches, battle queue, aftermath, Stillness, candle, wins and losses, event triggers |\n185\t| `23-ai-campaign.js` | the realm planner (§4.15) |\n186\t| `24-save.js` | the save format, versions, migration, the `game.saves` hookup |\n187\t| `30-art-palette.js` | realm and season palettes, `E.tones` helpers, sigils |\n188\t| `31-art-hexfield.js` | the battlefield: the hex floor baked through a `TileMap`'s `floorTex`, hex decals (range, path, Ward, cursor, danger), terrain props, raised hex blocks as shapes, season dressing, lights, weather |\n189\t| `32-art-worldmap.js` | the continent painted through `floorTex`, castles as `E.SHAPES`, roads, banners, troop figures, march arrows, season wipes |\n190\t| `33-art-units.js` | the unit visual factory: a Humanoid, Blob or Beast from a spec; move sets per class and species; portraits |\n191\t| `34-art-fx.js` | spell effects per element, status icons, summoning circle, capture chains, level-up and class-change flourishes, the bell clock face |\n192\t| `35-art-shapes.js` | new `E.SHAPES`: castles in five styles, keep, tower, rampart hex block, gate, clock tower, shrine, season dial, banner pole, ruins |\n193\t| `36-art-icons.js` | string sprites: element, size, item and status icons, sigils, PlayStation, Xbox and mouse glyphs, keycap frames |\n194\t| `40-audio-music.js` | every song (§9.1) |\n195\t| `41-audio-sfx.js` | custom sound effects (§9.2) |\n196\t| `42-audio-voice.js` | the babble voice synth and voice profiles (§9.3) |\n197\t| `50-ui-kit.js` | 10-foot widgets: `Panel`, `List`, `Grid`, `Card`, `Bars`, `Tabs`, `Slider`, `Toggle`, `PromptBar`, `Toast`, `Confirm`; the focus model (d-pad, stick, hover, click, wheel) |\n198\t| `51-ui-input.js` | the game's input presets (§8.2), glyph lookup per device, the PlayStation/Xbox setting, hot-seat input routing, idle tracking (attract), touch-only detection |\n199\t| `60-scene-title.js` | boot steps, title, the touch-only notice, the attract trigger |\n200\t| `61-scene-setup.js` | campaign setup (realm, players, devices, difficulty), skirmish setup |\n201\t| `62-scene-campaign.js` | the world map scene: Tidings, Muster panels, March, resolution |\n202\t| `63-scene-battle.js` | the battle scene: the camera, the player's commands, HUD, AI turns, event playback |\n203\t| `64-scene-event.js` | story stages, `E.Story` runner, emotes |\n204\t| `65-scene-results.js` | battle results, endings, credits roll |\n205\t| `66-scene-about.js` | About/Credits, How to Play |\n206\t| `67-scene-attract.js` | the attract reel |\n207\t| `70-dev.js` | the dev panel: tools, sliders, overlays, the gallery scene |\n208\t| `80-net-hub.js` | AI Hub session: connect, roles, protocol, timers, scoring, finish (§11) |\n209\t| `81-local-mp.js` | hot-seat seats, device binding, privacy screens |\n210\t| `90-main.js` | `new E.Game(...)`, menu items, settings, scenes, deep links, `game.debug`, `game.start` |\n211\t\n212\t### 3.3 What the engine provides (use it; don't rebuild it)\n213\t- `E.Game` with `res: 'ps1', expand: true` (320 × 240 at least; a 16:9 screen shows about 427 × 240), scenes, `go` with\n214\t  transitions, standard pause, settings, controls and rebinding, full screen, `game.saves`, boot steps, `game.debug`, and\n215\t  headless `game.advance`.\n216\t- `E.TileMap`, whose `floorTex` bakes the hex battlefield and the continent. `E.Grid(w, h, { hex: 'pointy' })` gives\n217\t  neighbours, distances, `path`, `reach` (with `blocked` and `pass`), `line` and `shape`.\n218\t- `E.Humanoid`, its moves (`E.MOVES`), poses, expressions and `drawPortrait`; `E.Blob` (slimes, wisps, bats); `r.actor`,\n219\t  `r.queue`, `r.decal`, `r.shape`, `r.prop`, `r.shadow`, `r.pick`.\n220\t- Particles, `game.hitFx`, `game.flash` and `game.shake` (comfort-aware), canvas lighting (plus `enableGPU({ map })` where\n221\t  WebGPU exists), weather, `E.Backdrop`.\n222\t- `E.Story` with `E.Dialog` (portraits, markup, choices, emotes, backlog); `E.Menu` for plain lists; `game.input`\n223\t  (presets, `label`, `glyphs`, `lastDevice`, players, `allowJoin`); `game.audio` (step-sequencer music, sfx, `ctx`);\n224\t  `game.tween`, `game.script`, `E.Path`, `E.rng`.\n225\t- Not used: physics, mode7, road, drive, fight, click, terrain, rooms (they stay in the engine file, unused).\n226\t\n227\t### 3.4 Planned engine changes (log each one in ENGINE-CHANGES.md)\n228\t1. **`E.Dialog` per-letter voice.** `voice` may be a function `(ch, index, talk)` called for each typed character, so\n229\t   Animalese voices can follow the text. The default `'text'` blip stays as it is.\n230\t2. **An effects bus.** Expose `game.audio.sfxBus`, a GainNode under the master gain, so custom Web Audio voices follow\n231\t   volume and mute. If the engine already has one under another name, use it and note that instead.\n232\t3. **`engine-ext/beasts.js`, a new module** written like an engine module with its manual in the header: `E.Beast` rigs\n233\t   (quadruped, drake, avian and serpent bodies) and `E.Wings` (insect, feather or bat wings drawn with a Humanoid), with\n234\t   the Humanoid's interface: `update(dt, state)`, `draw(g, ox, oy, view)`, `drawPortrait(g, x, y, size)`, plus\n235\t   `mouth()` and `tip()` points for breath and projectiles (§7.5).\n236\t4. **Only if missing:** registering a custom view by name so scenes can use `view: 'board'`, and input idle time for the\n237\t   attract mode. Check the pinball kit for how it uses `new E.View(...)`. Anything else found missing is fixed in place\n238\t   and logged.\n239\t\n240\t### 3.5 Saves, settings and tunables\n241\t- **A save** is `{ v, campaign: CampaignSim.serialize(), story: story.state(), meta: { realms, season, year, played } }`,\n242\t  stored through `game.saves.write` and `read` (3 slots plus the autosave; `menu: { saves: true }` with `version` and\n243\t  `migrate`). `saves.canSave` is true only on the campaign map during Muster. Autosave runs at every Tidings and before\n244\t  every battle the player commands.\n245\t- **Settings:** the engine's own plus the game's (§10.6).\n246\t- **Tunables:** `G.tune` is one live object the sims read every time (Appendix A). Dev mode edits it; changed values are\n247\t  kept under `E.store` key `brigantime:tune`; Reset copies `G.TUNE_DEFAULTS` back. Saves don't store tunables. Hub\n248\t  sessions always use the defaults.\n249\t\n250\t### 3.6 Scenes and deep links\n251\tScenes: `title`, `attract`, `setup`, `skirmishSetup`, `campaign`, `battle`, `event`, `results`, `ending`, `credits`,\n252\t`about`, `help`, `gallery` (dev), `hub` (session play). Gameplay scenes are `pausable: true`; title, attract, endings\n253\tand credits are not. Deep links (for tools, filmstrips and tests; ignored inside a hub session) are `#title`, `#attract`,\n254\t`#campaign/<realm>`, `#battle/<map>/<season>/<preset>`, `#event/<id>`, `#ending/<id>`, `#about`, `#help`, `#gallery`\n255\tand `#skirmish`.\n256\t\n257\t### 3.7 `game.debug` (the handle tests and tools read)\n258\t- `snapshot()` returns `{ scene, phase, season, year, candle, realm, mana, focus, panel, prompts: [labels shown],\n259\t  battle: { map, bell, troop, mode, cursor, units: [[id, side, cx, cy, hp, mp, status]], over }, event, touchOnly,\n260\t  attract: { beat }, errors }`.\n261\t- Helpers: `newCampaign({ realms, humans, difficulty, seed })`, `startBattle(spec)`, `sim()`, `cmd(c)`,\n262\t  `autoplay(on)`, `setTune(k, v)`, `resetTune()`, `trigger(eventId)`, `jump(season, year)`, `hash()`.\n263\t\n264\t---\n265\t\n266\t## 4. Campaign rules (`CampaignSim`)\n267\t\n268\t### 4.1 Calendar and the Hour-Candle\n269\t- One turn is one season: Spring → Summer → Autumn → Winter. The campaign opens in Spring of Year 1 (\"Spring, Year 1 of\n270\t  the Brigantime\").\n271\t- The event `candle_lit` (Spring, Year 2) lights the **Hour-Candle** with `tune.candleSeasons` seasons (Easy 16,\n272\t  Normal 12, Hard 10). Each later Tidings burns one. If a Tidings finds it at 0 and the Gray Knight still stands, the\n273\t  Thirteenth Month falls and the player loses. On Normal the last playable season is Winter of Year 4. The HUD shows the\n274\t  candle and the seasons left from the moment it is lit.\n275\t\n276\t### 4.2 Seasons outside battle\n277\t- **Income:** castles whose region's season is the current season yield +25% (`tune.seasonYield`).\n278\t- **Wintering:** in Winter, every non-Winter monster's upkeep is +25% (`tune.winterUpkeep`).\n279\t- **AI:** a realm in its own season has ×1.2 aggression.\n280\t- **Presentation:** the map recolours and its weather, music and Tidings banner change (\"WINTER. Hoarfast's knights grow\n281\t  bold.\").\n282\t- Effects inside battles are in §5.11.\n283\t\n284\t### 4.3 The three phases of a season\n285\t1. **Tidings** (automatic, one card at a time; confirm advances, hold to skip all):\n286\t   1. the season card (the Wheel turns to the new season, with that season's sting);\n287\t   2. the candle burns, if it is lit;\n288\t   3. recovery: wounds tick down, every unit at a castle returns to full HP and MP, and last season's Stilled castle\n289\t      wakes;\n290\t   4. Stillness picks a new castle (§4.16);\n291\t   5. quests return (§4.9);\n292\t   6. income minus upkeep, per realm (the report shows the player's own);\n293\t   7. story events (at most 3 scenes a Tidings; the rest wait for the next one, §6.12);\n294\t   8. the AI realms run their Muster instantly (their results are visible on the map).\n295\t2. **Muster** (each human realm in seat order; a privacy card between humans, §10.8): the actions in §4.5–4.11, in any\n296\t   order, as often as their limits allow. End with □ held for 0.6 s (Backspace on a keyboard) and a confirm.\n297\t3. **March:** each human declares attacks (§4.12), then each AI realm declares (hidden until resolution), then the\n298\t   battles resolve (§4.13). After the last aftermath, the season ends and the next Tidings begins.\n299\t\n300\t### 4.4 Castles\n301\tEach castle has `{ id, name, region (season or hour or neutral), tier, mana, circle: [species], site, map, mirror,\n302\ttreasury, pos: [x, y], roads: [ids] }` from data (§6.2) and `{ owner, knights: [ids], reserve: [monster ids], stilled,\n303\ttreasuryTaken }` in state. At most 6 knights can be stationed in one castle. Every player sees every castle's knights\n304\tand troops (the source hides nothing, and neither does this game).\n305\t\n306\t### 4.5 Mana\n307\t- **Yields per season:** capital 40, inner castle 25, flank castle 20, cross-quarter (brigand) castle 30, Hour satellite\n308\t  30, Meridian 60. All are scaled by `tune.manaIncome`.\n309\t- **Starting mana:** 120 for each season-realm and 300 for the Dominion. Brigands have no economy.\n310\t- **Each Tidings:** mana += income − Σ upkeep. If the result is below 0, monsters desert one at a time, the lowest power\n311\t  per point of upkeep first, until mana ≥ 0 (each loss is reported).\n312\t\n313\t### 4.6 Summon\n314\tAt an owned castle that is not Stilled, pick a tier-1 species from its circle (entries marked with a season can be\n315\tsummoned only in that season). Pay `cost × tune.summonCost`. The monster appears in the castle's reserve at level\n316\t`max(1, floor(average level of the realm's knights / 2))`. Each castle allows at most 3 summons per season. Upkeep is\n317\tcharged from the next Tidings.\n318\t\n319\t### 4.7 Bind (Organize)\n320\tMove monsters between a castle's reserve and the knights stationed there. A troop holds at most 6 monsters, and their\n321\tbond costs (rank, §6.8) may not add up to more than the knight's **Bond** (class base + floor(level / 4), plus items).\n322\tEach knight also has a **formation** (Vanguard, Shield or Column, §5.2). A reserve monster can be **dismissed** (it is\n323\tgone; ask first).\n324\t\n325\t### 4.8 Move\n326\tA knight with its troop (not wounded, not questing) may move once per Muster to an adjacent castle of its own realm,\n327\tbut not into or out of a Stilled castle. Moving does not stop it marching from its new castle.\n328\t\n329\t### 4.9 Quests\n330\t- **Who:** any knight who is not a ruler, not wounded and has not moved this Muster. It leaves with its troop for the"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"07d72fa0-3e0a-4abc-a856-6a3bd495de67","uuid":"8a2845f6-a613-4b30-bfad-8ea70f90dee6","timestamp":"2026-11-04T01:49:48.114Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174758__x42640e/work/PLAN.md","content":"# Brigantime: The Legend of Forseasons\n\nBuild plan for a feature-complete demo inspired by **Brigandine: The Legend of Forsena** (Hearty Robin, PlayStation;\nAtlus in North America, 1998). It is for the developers who will build the game from `TASK.md` and this file, who may be\nother AI models working alone. Every number here is a starting value that the dev-mode sliders can tune.\n\n**How the title was picked (as TASK.md asks):** I brainstormed 50 riffs on the source's name, then a real random number\ngenerator (Python's `secrets.randbelow(50)`, which draws from the OS) picked #21 on 2026-11-03 at 17:57:56. The title then\nshaped the design. *Brigan-time* is the name of an age of war and of a clock that is running out. *Forseasons* stands for\nfour seasons and four realms: the seasons drive the calendar, the elements, the terrain and the story.\n\n---\n\n## 0. At a glance\n\n**Pitch.** The Keeper of the Wheel sleeps in Meridian, and the Great Clock that turns the seasons is failing. Four\nseason-realms, Verdance (Spring), Solmark (Summer), Amberlyn (Autumn) and Hoarfast (Winter), race one another and the\nusurping Hourless Dominion to reach the Calendar City before the Thirteenth Month, when the Gray Knight will stop time\nforever. The player picks a realm and musters Wheelsworn knights who bind monsters to their banners. Castles yield the\nmana to summon beasts. Knights go questing, and armies march on neighbouring castles season by season. Every attack\nbecomes a turn-based hex battle of up to three troops a side, fought within twelve bells. Each season changes the land\n(spring floods, summer fires, autumn gales, winter ice) and the strength of every army.\n\n**Core loop (one sentence, write it at the top of `src/00-core.js`):** each season, read the tidings, muster your castles\n(summon, bind, move, quest, equip, promote), declare your marches, then fight the battles they cause, growing knights and\nmonsters until you can take Meridian and defeat the Gray Knight before the Hour-Candle burns out.\n\n**Genre:** turn-based grand strategy with a tactical RPG battle layer.\n\n**Perspective:**\n- Campaign: a three-quarter overhead diorama of the continent (the engine's `threequarter` view, north up, pan and zoom).\n- Battles: a tilted overhead \"board\" view of a hex battlefield (a custom `E.View`, yaw 0, pitch 50), rotatable in 60°\n  steps and zoomable, with the engine's `iso` view as the alternate (`V`).\n- Story events: side-on stage scenes (the engine's `brawler` view) with live portraits in the dialog box.\n- Title and attract mode: a diorama of the four seasons around Meridian's clock tower.\n\n**Scope (all of it ships complete, with nothing stubbed):**\n\n| Content | Count |\n|---|---|\n| Continent / castles / roads | 1 (Forseason) / 25 / 36 |\n| Realms | 4 playable + the Hourless Dominion (AI only) + 4 neutral brigand castles |\n| Knights | 36: 4 playable rulers, 16 realm knights, 6 Dominion knights (ruler included), the Gray Knight, 5 free knights, 4 brigand captains |\n| Knight classes | 23: 5 lines × 3 tiers, plus 8 unique classes |\n| Monster species | 30, each with 3 forms (tier 1 → 2 → 3) |\n| Skills and spells / effects / items | 90 / 11 / 33 |\n| Battle maps | 13 hand-made hex maps (all checked by script), dressed per region and changed per season |\n| Story | about 40 events, 4 good endings, 3 kinds of bad ending, a tutorial battle |\n| Modes | Campaign (1–4 players, hot-seat), Skirmish (2–4 sides, local humans or CPU), AI Hub shared-world skirmish (2–4 online), attract mode, dev mode |\n\nA campaign lasts at most 16 seasons. A first win on Normal should take 2–3 hours with battles played by hand, or about\n45 minutes with battles delegated.\n\n**Deliverables (exact names):**\n- Dev folder: `BrigantimeTheLegendOfForseasons-BrigandineTheLegendOfForsena-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174758/` (called\n  `<DEV>` below). GameTitle and SOURCE are written in PascalCase with the punctuation removed.\n- The game: `BrigantimeTheLegendOfForseasons-BrigandineTheLegendOfForsena-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174758.html`, one\n  self-contained file in this folder's root, beside `TASK.md`.\n- `exit-interview.json` in this folder's root, in TASK.md's exact shape (§15).\n- The About/Credits screen and the final chat reply carry Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt\n  Feedback (§10.4, §15). Plan: `PLAN.md`.\n\n---\n\n## 1. From the source to this game\n\n### 1.1 What makes Brigandine distinctive, and what it becomes here\n\n| Source trait | Mechanic in Brigantime |\n|---|---|\n| Months of war, each with an Organize phase and an Attack phase | Seasons (Spring → Summer → Autumn → Winter), each with **Tidings** (news), **Muster** (organize) and **March** (attack) |\n| A continent of castles held by rival nations; pick one and conquer | Forseason: 25 castles on a wheel-shaped map, 4 playable season-realms, the Dominion at the hub, brigand castles between |\n| Rune Knights lead troops of monsters; Rune Power limits how many | **Wheelsworn knights** lead a troop of up to 6 monsters; a knight's **Bond** caps the troop's total bond cost |\n| Castles make mana; summoning costs mana; monsters cost upkeep | The same, plus seasonal yields and a **summon circle** per castle (taking castles unlocks new species) |\n| Quests: a knight leaves for a while; mostly random help or harm | Quests to named sites with an outcome table (treasure, beasts, wanderers, mana, training, mishap) and short vignettes |\n| Hex battles, one unit per hex, up to 3 troops a side; the attacker has 12 turns | Hex battles of up to 12 **bells** (a clock face counts them); an attacker knight on the Keep at the last bell also wins |\n| A troop is beaten when its leader falls or retreats; a fallen ruler routs the nation | The same rules |\n| Rune Area: monsters outside it weaken and are easier to capture | **Ward**: a radius around each knight; outside it monsters lose 20% ATK, DEF and INT and are easier to capture |\n| Monsters at 0 HP die for good; knights are out for a month | Monsters at 0 HP die for good; knights are wounded and miss the next season |\n| Elements red, blue, green, white and black, with opposites | **Seasons are the elements**: Spring opposes Autumn, Summer opposes Winter; **Hour** (the enemy's timeless element) stands apart |\n| Scale: big monsters struggle to hit small, quick ones | Size classes S, M and L change hit chance (a dragon swinging at a pixie: −30%) |\n| Spells can't be cast after moving; some special attacks can | The same: spells need planted feet; specials marked *mobile* work after moving |\n| Knights change class; monsters promote | 5 class lines × 3 tiers plus unique ruler classes; every species has 3 forms |\n| Brief, earnest story vignettes between turns, a path per nation | The same, with choices that set flags, recruit knights or change the AI |\n| Grand Edition: up to 6 human nations; a final boss after conquest | Hot-seat for 1–4 humans; AI Hub online skirmish; the Gray Knight in the Clockwork Sanctum |\n| *New, from the title* | **Seasonal Tide** (the season's element hits harder), **seasonal terrain** (floods, fires, gales, ice), the **Hour-Candle** deadline, the **Season Dials** puzzle in the final battle |\n\n### 1.2 Originality guardrails\n- The title riff is the only nod to the source's name. Never use the source's proper names: Forsena, Almekia, Esgares,\n  Norgard, Caerleon, Iscalio, Leonia, Padstow, Lance, Zemeckis, Cador, Henguist, Vaynard, Lyonesse, Dryst, Cai, or any\n  other source character, castle or monster name.\n- The world, map, characters, plot, art, music and UI layouts are our own. Knight names come from public-domain\n  Arthurian legend or are invented, which matches the source's Arthurian flavour without copying its cast.\n- Monsters are generic folklore types (wolves, dragons, golems, pixies) drawn by our own rigs.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Folders, build and the working environment\n\n```\n<this folder>/                      TASK.md, STAGE.md, PLAN.md, the built .html, exit-interview.json\n  <DEV>/\n    engine/        a copy of my-3d2dge dist/handoff (v0.85.2): my-3d2dge.js (full engine, every module), modules/,\n                   tools/ (check, filmstrip, replay, asset-*), docs/ASSETS.md, kits/ (reference only), package.json\n    engine-ext/    new engine-level code: beasts.js (the Beast rig module, §7.5); changes to engine/ are edits in place\n    vendor/hub/    AI Hub: session.js, harness.html, bridge.js, round.js, copied unmodified\n    src/           the game: numbered files, concatenated in order into one IIFE (§3.2)\n    tests/         run.mjs (the one test command) and the suites t-*.mjs\n    tools/         build.mjs, simulate.mjs (balance runs), hub-check.mjs (harness driver)\n    dist/          build output: game.js, index.html (the dev page), hub/ (harness files + a copy of the game)\n    ENGINE-CHANGES.md   every change to engine/ or engine-ext/, and why (it feeds Engine Feedback)\n```\n\n- **Get the engine:** `git clone --depth 1 --filter=blob:none --sparse https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge.git` into\n  a new empty directory, then `git sparse-checkout set dist/handoff`, then copy `dist/handoff/*` into `<DEV>/engine/`. Read\n  its `README.md` and `API.md` whole; read the parts of `AI_GUIDE.md` named here; grep `API-INDEX.md` for names. Do the\n  same for `https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub` with `lib/hub`, copied into `<DEV>/vendor/hub/`, and read its\n  `README.md` whole.\n- **Build (`node tools/build.mjs`):**\n  1. Concatenate `src/*.js` in name order inside `(() => { 'use strict'; const E = My3D2dge, G = {}; ... })();` and write\n     `dist/game.js`. Each file attaches what it defines to `G`; there are no other globals.\n  2. Write `dist/index.html`, which loads `../engine/my-3d2dge.js`, `../engine-ext/beasts.js` and `game.js`, for\n     development.\n  3. Write the single file in this order: `<!doctype html><html lang=\"en\"><head><meta charset=\"utf-8\">`, then\n     `<script>` holding `vendor/hub/session.js` verbatim (the first script, unmodified), then the hub meta tags (§11.2),\n     the viewport meta, `<title>Brigantime: The Legend of Forseasons</title>` and the page style. The `<body>` holds the\n     `E.bootSplash('BRIGANTIME')` markup and `<canvas id=\"screen\">`, then one `<script>` each for the engine,\n     engine-ext and the game. Escape every `</script` inside inlined code as `<\\/script`.\n  4. Fail the build if `src/` contains `TODO`, `FIXME`, `XXX`, `placeholder` or `lorem`, or if any asset URL is not a\n     data URI.\n- **Play:** open the single HTML file from `file://`. During development, open `dist/index.html`.\n- **This machine:** at planning time `/tmp` was a full 9.8 GB tmpfs with 0 MB free, so command output written there\n  gets lost. Set `TMPDIR=<DEV>/.tmp` before running Playwright or Chromium, and send long command output to files on the\n  main disk. Delete nothing in `/tmp` that you did not create.\n- **Servers** (only the hub harness needs one): run `python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` from `<DEV>/dist/hub/`\n  and read the port it prints. Fetch the game page and check that its `<title>` is ours before trusting a result. Stop\n  the server by its own PID only; never use `pkill`, `killall`, `fuser -k`, or kill whatever holds a port. Other agents\n  share this machine and directory tree, so read only your own folder and the engine and hub sources.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Architecture\n\n### 3.1 Layers\n\n```\n data registries ──► simulations (pure, deterministic, JSON state) ──► events ──► presentation (scenes, art, audio, UI)\n        ▲                     ▲                                                        │\n        │                     └──── commands ◄── AI (campaign, battle) / input (pad, keys, mouse) / net (AI Hub)\n```\n\n- **Data.** Every content item is registered once with `G.def(kind, id, spec)`, then read with `G.get(kind, id)` and\n  `G.all(kind)`. The kinds are `element, terrain, effect, skill, class, monster, knight, item, castle, map, site, event,\n  realm, song, voice`. A validation suite checks that every id a spec refers to exists. Content never edits the code\n  that runs it.\n- **Simulations.** `G.BattleSim` and `G.CampaignSim` keep their state as plain JSON. They never touch the engine, the\n  DOM, the clock or `Math.random`. Each one offers `legal(...)`, `apply(cmd) → events[]`, `forecast(...)` (battle),\n  `serialize()`, `G.*Sim.load(json)` and `hash()`. Randomness comes from `G.Rng` (mulberry32), whose state lives inside\n  the sim state, so the same seed and the same commands always give the same events. Tests assert this.\n- **AI.** Pure functions turn a sim state into commands. They serve CPU realms, Delegate (auto-resolve), the attract\n  mode, the hub host for players who time out, and the playthrough bot.\n- **Presentation.** Engine scenes read sim state and play the event stream through `G.EventPlayer`: a queue in which each\n  event type has a player that animates it and reports when it is done. Scenes turn input into commands. Presentation\n  never writes sim state directly, so the battle the player watches is the same battle auto-resolve would compute.\n- **Net.** The AI Hub layer wraps `BattleSim` in a protocol where the host's copy is the authority (§11).\n\n### 3.2 Source files (concatenated in this order)\n\n| File | Holds |\n|---|---|\n| `00-core.js` | `G` namespace, the core-loop line, `G.def/get/all`, `G.Rng`, hex helpers (`G.hex.center`, `toCell`, `corners`, `edgeDist`, `dirTo`, screen-direction → neighbour), the event bus `G.on/emit`, `G.tune` (live tunables) and `G.TUNE_DEFAULTS` |\n| `01-data-rules.js` | elements and their relations, seasons and the Tide, terrain types and season transforms, size table, effects (statuses, buffs, debuffs) |\n| `02-data-skills.js` | every skill and spell (§6.7) |\n| `03-data-classes.js` | knight classes, growth, class changes (§6.6) |\n| `04-data-monsters.js` | the bestiary: 30 species × 3 forms, rig specs, voices (§6.8) |\n| `05-data-items.js` | items (§6.9) |\n| `06-data-knights.js` | every knight: class, level, element, look, voice, personality (§6.5) |\n| `07-data-world.js` | realms, castles, roads, circles, sites, starting state (§6.1–6.4, §6.11) |\n| `08-data-maps.js` | the 13 battle maps as ASCII rows plus their dressing (§6.10) |\n| `09-data-story.js` | events: triggers, conditions, `E.Story` nodes, effects, endings, rival lines (§6.12) |\n| `10-data-about.js` | the About/Credits text: Source Information and the three feedback lists (§10.4) |\n| `20-sim-battle.js` | `BattleSim`: setup and deployment, turn order, legality, actions, formulas, Ward, capture, bells, Keep, wins, EXP and levels, forecast |\n| `21-ai-battle.js` | the battle planner (§5.13) and auto-resolve |\n| `22-sim-campaign.js` | `CampaignSim`: calendar, phases, economy, summon, bind, move, quest, equip, promote, marches, battle queue, aftermath, Stillness, candle, wins and losses, event triggers |\n| `23-ai-campaign.js` | the realm planner (§4.15) |\n| `24-save.js` | the save format, versions, migration, the `game.saves` hookup |\n| `30-art-palette.js` | realm and season palettes, `E.tones` helpers, sigils |\n| `31-art-hexfield.js` | the battlefield: the hex floor baked through a `TileMap`'s `floorTex`, hex decals (range, path, Ward, cursor, danger), terrain props, raised hex blocks as shapes, season dressing, lights, weather |\n| `32-art-worldmap.js` | the continent painted through `floorTex`, castles as `E.SHAPES`, roads, banners, troop figures, march arrows, season wipes |\n| `33-art-units.js` | the unit visual factory: a Humanoid, Blob or Beast from a spec; move sets per class and species; portraits |\n| `34-art-fx.js` | spell effects per element, status icons, summoning circle, capture chains, level-up and class-change flourishes, the bell clock face |\n| `35-art-shapes.js` | new `E.SHAPES`: castles in five styles, keep, tower, rampart hex block, gate, clock tower, shrine, season dial, banner pole, ruins |\n| `36-art-icons.js` | string sprites: element, size, item and status icons, sigils, PlayStation, Xbox and mouse glyphs, keycap frames |\n| `40-audio-music.js` | every song (§9.1) |\n| `41-audio-sfx.js` | custom sound effects (§9.2) |\n| `42-audio-voice.js` | the babble voice synth and voice profiles (§9.3) |\n| `50-ui-kit.js` | 10-foot widgets: `Panel`, `List`, `Grid`, `Card`, `Bars`, `Tabs`, `Slider`, `Toggle`, `PromptBar`, `Toast`, `Confirm`; the focus model (d-pad, stick, hover, click, wheel) |\n| `51-ui-input.js` | the game's input presets (§8.2), glyph lookup per device, the PlayStation/Xbox setting, hot-seat input routing, idle tracking (attract), touch-only detection |\n| `60-scene-title.js` | boot steps, title, the touch-only notice, the attract trigger |\n| `61-scene-setup.js` | campaign setup (realm, players, devices, difficulty), skirmish setup |\n| `62-scene-campaign.js` | the world map scene: Tidings, Muster panels, March, resolution |\n| `63-scene-battle.js` | the battle scene: the camera, the player's commands, HUD, AI turns, event playback |\n| `64-scene-event.js` | story stages, `E.Story` runner, emotes |\n| `65-scene-results.js` | battle results, endings, credits roll |\n| `66-scene-about.js` | About/Credits, How to Play |\n| `67-scene-attract.js` | the attract reel |\n| `70-dev.js` | the dev panel: tools, sliders, overlays, the gallery scene |\n| `80-net-hub.js` | AI Hub session: connect, roles, protocol, timers, scoring, finish (§11) |\n| `81-local-mp.js` | hot-seat seats, device binding, privacy screens |\n| `90-main.js` | `new E.Game(...)`, menu items, settings, scenes, deep links, `game.debug`, `game.start` |\n\n### 3.3 What the engine provides (use it; don't rebuild it)\n- `E.Game` with `res: 'ps1', expand: true` (320 × 240 at least; a 16:9 screen shows about 427 × 240), scenes, `go` with\n  transitions, standard pause, settings, controls and rebinding, full screen, `game.saves`, boot steps, `game.debug`, and\n  headless `game.advance`.\n- `E.TileMap`, whose `floorTex` bakes the hex battlefield and the continent. `E.Grid(w, h, { hex: 'pointy' })` gives\n  neighbours, distances, `path`, `reach` (with `blocked` and `pass`), `line` and `shape`.\n- `E.Humanoid`, its moves (`E.MOVES`), poses, expressions and `drawPortrait`; `E.Blob` (slimes, wisps, bats); `r.actor`,\n  `r.queue`, `r.decal`, `r.shape`, `r.prop`, `r.shadow`, `r.pick`.\n- Particles, `game.hitFx`, `game.flash` and `game.shake` (comfort-aware), canvas lighting (plus `enableGPU({ map })` where\n  WebGPU exists), weather, `E.Backdrop`.\n- `E.Story` with `E.Dialog` (portraits, markup, choices, emotes, backlog); `E.Menu` for plain lists; `game.input`\n  (presets, `label`, `glyphs`, `lastDevice`, players, `allowJoin`); `game.audio` (step-sequencer music, sfx, `ctx`);\n  `game.tween`, `game.script`, `E.Path`, `E.rng`.\n- Not used: physics, mode7, road, drive, fight, click, terrain, rooms (they stay in the engine file, unused).\n\n### 3.4 Planned engine changes (log each one in ENGINE-CHANGES.md)\n1. **`E.Dialog` per-letter voice.** `voice` may be a function `(ch, index, talk)` called for each typed character, so\n   Animalese voices can follow the text. The default `'text'` blip stays as it is.\n2. **An effects bus.** Expose `game.audio.sfxBus`, a GainNode under the master gain, so custom Web Audio voices follow\n   volume and mute. If the engine already has one under another name, use it and note that instead.\n3. **`engine-ext/beasts.js`, a new module** written like an engine module with its manual in the header: `E.Beast` rigs\n   (quadruped, drake, avian and serpent bodies) and `E.Wings` (insect, feather or bat wings drawn with a Humanoid), with\n   the Humanoid's interface: `update(dt, state)`, `draw(g, ox, oy, view)`, `drawPortrait(g, x, y, size)`, plus\n   `mouth()` and `tip()` points for breath and projectiles (§7.5).\n4. **Only if missing:** registering a custom view by name so scenes can use `view: 'board'`, and input idle time for the\n   attract mode. Check the pinball kit for how it uses `new E.View(...)`. Anything else found missing is fixed in place\n   and logged.\n\n### 3.5 Saves, settings and tunables\n- **A save** is `{ v, campaign: CampaignSim.serialize(), story: story.state(), meta: { realms, season, year, played } }`,\n  stored through `game.saves.write` and `read` (3 slots plus the autosave; `menu: { saves: true }` with `version` and\n  `migrate`). `saves.canSave` is true only on the campaign map during Muster. Autosave runs at every Tidings and before\n  every battle the player commands.\n- **Settings:** the engine's own plus the game's (§10.6).\n- **Tunables:** `G.tune` is one live object the sims read every time (Appendix A). Dev mode edits it; changed values are\n  kept under `E.store` key `brigantime:tune`; Reset copies `G.TUNE_DEFAULTS` back. Saves don't store tunables. Hub\n  sessions always use the defaults.\n\n### 3.6 Scenes and deep links\nScenes: `title`, `attract`, `setup`, `skirmishSetup`, `campaign`, `battle`, `event`, `results`, `ending`, `credits`,\n`about`, `help`, `gallery` (dev), `hub` (session play). Gameplay scenes are `pausable: true`; title, attract, endings\nand credits are not. Deep links (for tools, filmstrips and tests; ignored inside a hub session) are `#title`, `#attract`,\n`#campaign/<realm>`, `#battle/<map>/<season>/<preset>`, `#event/<id>`, `#ending/<id>`, `#about`, `#help`, `#gallery`\nand `#skirmish`.\n\n### 3.7 `game.debug` (the handle tests and tools read)\n- `snapshot()` returns `{ scene, phase, season, year, candle, realm, mana, focus, panel, prompts: [labels shown],\n  battle: { map, bell, troop, mode, cursor, units: [[id, side, cx, cy, hp, mp, status]], over }, event, touchOnly,\n  attract: { beat }, errors }`.\n- Helpers: `newCampaign({ realms, humans, difficulty, seed })`, `startBattle(spec)`, `sim()`, `cmd(c)`,\n  `autoplay(on)`, `setTune(k, v)`, `resetTune()`, `trigger(eventId)`, `jump(season, year)`, `hash()`.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Campaign rules (`CampaignSim`)\n\n### 4.1 Calendar and the Hour-Candle\n- One turn is one season: Spring → Summer → Autumn → Winter. The campaign opens in Spring of Year 1 (\"Spring, Year 1 of\n  the Brigantime\").\n- The event `candle_lit` (Spring, Year 2) lights the **Hour-Candle** with `tune.candleSeasons` seasons (Easy 16,\n  Normal 12, Hard 10). Each later Tidings burns one. If a Tidings finds it at 0 and the Gray Knight still stands, the\n  Thirteenth Month falls and the player loses. On Normal the last playable season is Winter of Year 4. The HUD shows the\n  candle and the seasons left from the moment it is lit.\n\n### 4.2 Seasons outside battle\n- **Income:** castles whose region's season is the current season yield +25% (`tune.seasonYield`).\n- **Wintering:** in Winter, every non-Winter monster's upkeep is +25% (`tune.winterUpkeep`).\n- **AI:** a realm in its own season has ×1.2 aggression.\n- **Presentation:** the map recolours and its weather, music and Tidings banner change (\"WINTER. Hoarfast's knights grow\n  bold.\").\n- Effects inside battles are in §5.11.\n\n### 4.3 The three phases of a season\n1. **Tidings** (automatic, one card at a time; confirm advances, hold to skip all):\n   1. the season card (the Wheel turns to the new season, with that season's sting);\n   2. the candle burns, if it is lit;\n   3. recovery: wounds tick down, every unit at a castle returns to full HP and MP, and last season's Stilled castle\n      wakes;\n   4. Stillness picks a new castle (§4.16);\n   5. quests return (§4.9);\n   6. income minus upkeep, per realm (the report shows the player's own);\n   7. story events (at most 3 scenes a Tidings; the rest wait for the next one, §6.12);\n   8. the AI realms run their Muster instantly (their results are visible on the map).\n2. **Muster** (each human realm in seat order; a privacy card between humans, §10.8): the actions in §4.5–4.11, in any\n   order, as often as their limits allow. End with □ held for 0.6 s (Backspace on a keyboard) and a confirm.\n3. **March:** each human declares attacks (§4.12), then each AI realm declares (hidden until resolution), then the\n   battles resolve (§4.13). After the last aftermath, the season ends and the next Tidings begins.\n\n### 4.4 Castles\nEach castle has `{ id, name, region (season or hour or neutral), tier, mana, circle: [species], site, map, mirror,\ntreasury, pos: [x, y], roads: [ids] }` from data (§6.2) and `{ owner, knights: [ids], reserve: [monster ids], stilled,\ntreasuryTaken }` in state. At most 6 knights can be stationed in one castle. Every player sees every castle's knights\nand troops (the source hides nothing, and neither does this game).\n\n### 4.5 Mana\n- **Yields per season:** capital 40, inner castle 25, flank castle 20, cross-quarter (brigand) castle 30, Hour satellite\n  30, Meridian 60. All are scaled by `tune.manaIncome`.\n- **Starting mana:** 120 for each season-realm and 300 for the Dominion. Brigands have no economy.\n- **Each Tidings:** mana += income − Σ upkeep. If the result is below 0, monsters desert one at a time, the lowest power\n  per point of upkeep first, until mana ≥ 0 (each loss is reported).\n\n### 4.6 Summon\nAt an owned castle that is not Stilled, pick a tier-1 species from its circle (entries marked with a season can be\nsummoned only in that season). Pay `cost × tune.summonCost`. The monster appears in the castle's reserve at level\n`max(1, floor(average level of the realm's knights / 2))`. Each castle allows at most 3 summons per season. Upkeep is\ncharged from the next Tidings.\n\n### 4.7 Bind (Organize)\nMove monsters between a castle's reserve and the knights stationed there. A troop holds at most 6 monsters, and their\nbond costs (rank, §6.8) may not add up to more than the knight's **Bond** (class base + floor(level / 4), plus items).\nEach knight also has a **formation** (Vanguard, Shield or Column, §5.2). A reserve monster can be **dismissed** (it is\ngone; ask first).\n\n### 4.8 Move\nA knight with its troop (not wounded, not questing) may move once per Muster to an adjacent castle of its own realm,\nbut not into or out of a Stilled castle. Moving does not stop it marching from its new castle.\n\n### 4.9 Quests\n- **Who:** any knight who is not a ruler, not wounded and has not moved this Muster. It leaves with its troop for the","numLines":330,"startLine":1,"totalLines":2404}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01Fj2Paztwvof52UsiH2Sy17","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
